## Free NHS sight tests

You can get a free NHS sight test if you:

- are **under 16**
- are **16, 17 or 18 and in full-time education**
- are **60 or over**
- are registered as **sight impaired** or **severely sight impaired**
- have **diabetes** or **glaucoma**
- are **40 or over** and your **parent, brother, sister or child** has glaucoma
- have been told by an eye doctor that you are **at risk of glaucoma**
- need **complex lenses**
- get **income-related ESA**, **Pension Credit Guarantee Credit**, or **Universal Credit** within the earnings limits
- have an **HC2** certificate (an **HC3** may help with part of the cost)

## Home visits

If you qualify for a free sight test and **cannot leave home without help** because of illness or disability, you can ask for a **free NHS home visit**. Contact a local optician that offers them.

## Vouchers for glasses or contact lenses

An **NHS optical voucher** pays towards glasses or contact lenses. You can get one if you:

- are **under 16**, or **16 to 18 and in full-time education**
- get **income-related ESA**, **Pension Credit Guarantee Credit**, or **Universal Credit** within the earnings limits
- have an **HC2** certificate (an **HC3** may give you a smaller voucher)
- need **complex lenses**

Being over 60 on its own does **not** give you a voucher. The value of the voucher depends on your prescription. You can use it for any glasses, and pay the difference if they cost more.

## In Wales

The rules are similar. Wales also offers **free eye examinations** through its eye health service for some people who have an **eye problem that needs attention**, or who are at **higher risk** of eye disease. Ask your optometrist whether you qualify.

